--TEST--
`$uri->compareTo` applies RFC 3986 § 5.2.4 to an *absolute* path and to nothing else: the same six rewrites all hold with a leading `/` and none of them holds without one, a query and a fragment keep their dot segments, and § 5.2.4's two trailing cases leave the path ending in `/` — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 6.2.2.3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::compareTo
// § 6.2.2.3 is the one of § 6.2.2's three rewrites with a *precondition*, and
// the precondition is the whole content: a relative reference's leading `..`
// is meaningful until something resolves it, so folding it away would answer
// that `../a` and `a` are one URI when they are two references to two
// different resources. That is a claim about a boundary, so both sides of it
// are counted over the same six rewrites rather than shown once each.

final class Same {
    public static function uris(string $a, string $b): int {
        return Core\Uri::parse($a)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($b));
    }
}

// The six rewrites, written as the path an author would write and the path
// § 5.2.4 leaves. Under an authority every path is absolute, so all six hold.
array<string> $written = [
    "http://h/a/../b",
    "http://h/a/./b",
    "http://h/a/b/../../c",
    "http://h/../a",
    "http://h/a/..",
    "http://h/a/.",
];
array<string> $folded = [
    "http://h/b",
    "http://h/a/b",
    "http://h/c",
    "http://h/a",
    "http://h/",
    "http://h/a/",
];
int $at = 0;
int $equal = 0;
foreach ($written as string $long) {
    if (Same::uris($long, $folded[$at as string]) == 0) { $equal = $equal + 1; }
    $at = $at + 1;
}
echo "absolute paths folded ", $equal, " of 6\n";

// The identical six with the leading `/` taken off, which is § 4.1's relative
// reference: none of them holds. A member that ran `remove_dot_segments` over
// every path would print `6` here and stay plausible on every row above.
array<string> $relative = [
    "a/../b",
    "a/./b",
    "a/b/../../c",
    "../a",
    "a/..",
    "a/.",
];
array<string> $shortened = [
    "b",
    "a/b",
    "c",
    "a",
    "",
    "a/",
];
int $next = 0;
int $stillEqual = 0;
foreach ($relative as string $long) {
    if (Same::uris($long, $shortened[$next as string]) == 0) { $stillEqual = $stillEqual + 1; }
    $next = $next + 1;
}
echo "relative paths folded ", $stillEqual, " of 6\n";

// A scheme does not make a path absolute — § 3.3's path-rootless is what
// `http:a/../b` has — so the precondition is the leading `/` and not the
// presence of a scheme or an authority. Named beside the path-absolute
// spelling that differs from it by exactly that byte.
echo "rootless ", Same::uris("http:a/../b", "http:b"),
     " path-absolute ", Same::uris("http:/a/../b", "http:/b"), "\n";

// The other axis of the same precondition: § 6.2.2.3 is about the *path*, so
// a query and a fragment keep every dot segment they were written with, even
// though the bytes are identical to the path row that folds.
echo "query ", Same::uris("http://h/p?a/../b", "http://h/p?b"),
     " fragment ", Same::uris("http://h/p#a/../b", "http://h/p#b"), "\n";

// Two things a segment list makes easy to get wrong, both of them § 5.2.4's
// own steps rather than anything this member invents. A trailing `.` or `..`
// appends an empty segment, which is why the two rows above pair with paths
// ending in `/` — `/a/..` is `/` and not the empty path, and `/a/.` is `/a/`
// and not `/a`. And an *empty interior* segment is not a dot segment at all,
// so `/a//b` keeps it.
echo "trailing ", Same::uris("http://h/a/..", "http://h"),
     " interior ", Same::uris("http://h/a//b", "http://h/a/b"), "\n";

// Climbing above the root is a no-op rather than an error: § 5.2.4 step 2C
// pops an empty buffer and carries on, so a path of nothing but `..` is `/`.
echo "above ", Same::uris("http://h/../../..", "http://h/"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
absolute paths folded 6 of 6
relative paths folded 0 of 6
rootless -1 path-absolute 0
query -1 fragment -1
trailing 1 interior -1
above 0
